a the Euler solver with 40 steps. This fixed-step solver provides a more deterministic evaluation protocol and allows a fair comparison of the computational cost across methods. For qualitative visualizations, we instead use the Dormand–Prince solver ( Dormand and Prince, 1980 ) ( dopri5 ) to obtain smoother trajectories and higher-quality visual results. Methods. We compare CounterFlow 2 2 2 The hyperparameters of CounterFlow are listed in Appendix. against three recent counterfactual generation methods: C3LT ( Khorram and Fuxin, 2022 ) , FastDiME ( Weng et al., 2024 ) , and LeapFactual ( Cao et al., 2025 ) , where we use the official implementations. Among diffusion-guidance methods, we retain only FastDiME as a representative baseline: DiME and ACE are prohibitively expensive to run at scale due to their inner-loop or per-step attack computations; DVCE requires an adversarially robust classifier, a constraint that our method does not rely on and that we therefore do not impose in our experiments; and MaskDiME’s masking mechanism restricts edits to a localized region, which conflicts with our evaluation setting where the change may need to affect the whole image. Beyond this choice, neither of the two remaining baselines, namely LeapFactual and FastDiME, is applicable to both datasets. LeapFactual relies on a compact VAE latent space to remain tractable, which does not scale to CIFAR-10 without dominant reconstruction artifacts and a much costlier latent flow, so we evaluate it only on MNIST. Conversely, FastDiME depends on pretrained OpenAI diffusion models built for natural images, so we evaluate it only on CIFAR-10. Models to be explained. To ensure a fair comparison, all methods rely on the same pretrained image classifier for a given dataset. For MNIST, we trained a simple convolutional neural network achieving an accuracy of 99.9 % 99.9\% on the test set. For CIFAR-10, we use a pretrained RepVGG-A2 classifier ( Ding et al., 2021 ) with publicly available weights from the pytorch-cifar-models repository (distributed through the official PyTorch Hub interface), achieving a test accuracy of 94.8 % 94.8\% . Both classifiers are frozen during training of counterfactual models and are used consistently for counterfactual generation and metrics computation across all competing methods. Metrics. We evaluate the counterfactual explanations along four axes: effectiveness (reaching f ⁡ ( 𝐱 1 ) = y 1 f(\mathbf{x}_{1})=y_{1} ), proximity to the source (a distribution-level counterpart of the distance d ⁡ ( 𝐱 1 , 𝐱 0 ) d(\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{0}) minimized per example in Equation 1 , measured in a semantic feature space rather than in a pixel space), realism (the plausibility constraint q y 1 ​ ( 𝐱 1 ) ≥ ε q_{y_{1}}(\mathbf{x}_{1})\geq\varepsilon , also from Equation 1 ), and computational efficiency . We operationalize the evaluation of these axes through the following five metrics. • Conditional Validity measures the proportion (in %) of generated counterfactuals x 1 x_{1} that are classified as the desired target class y 1 y_{1} , i.e. , whether we have f ⁡ ( 𝐱 1 ) = y 1 f(\mathbf{x}_{1})=y_{1} . • FID t computes the Fréchet Inception Distance between the generated counterfactuals and real samples from the target class, quantifying realism : lower values indicate that the generated samples better match the distribution of the desired class. • FID s computes the Fréchet Inception Distance between the generated counterfactuals and the original source class, measuring proximity to the source : lower values indicate that the counterfactuals remain closer to the original data distribution, suggesting that fewer semantic changes were applied to reach the target class. • FLOPs / sample measures the computational cost required to generate a single counterfactual explanation. It includes the complete inference pipeline, i.e. , all neural network evaluations required by the ODE solver. This metric provides an implementation-independent estimate of inference complexity. • Model Size reports the total number of trainable parameters of each method, allowing comparison of their memory footprint and model complexity. Following the standard definition of the Fréchet Inception Distance ( Heusel et al., 2017 , FID,) , feature statistics for FID t and FID s are extracted from pretrained image classification networks. For CIFAR-10, we use the pretrained Inception-V3 network available in torchvision . Since ImageNet-pretrained models are not well suited for handwritten digit recognition, for MNIST we instead compute features using the pretrained convolutional classifier employed throughout our experiments. FID is then computed between the Gaussian statistics of the extracted features from the generated counterfactuals and those of the corresponding source or target class, i.e. , FID t on the target class and FID s on the source class. Note that, since these statistics are estimated from the subset of test examples belonging to a single class, rather than from the full test set as in standard, unconditional FID computations, the reported values are higher than those typically observed in unconditional image generation. We therefore focus on relative comparisons between methods under the same evaluation protocol rather than on the absolute reported numbers. Table 1 : (a) Quantitative results and (b) Ablation of the pairing used to train CounterFlow: latent-space nearest neighbor ( Algorithm 1 ) vs. random. Higher conditional validity (CV) is better, while lower FID scores indicate that the generated counterfactuals are closer to the target or source distributions. Lower FLOPs/sample and model size (MS) are preferred for computational efficiency. Method CV ↑ \uparrow FID t ↓ \downarrow FID s ↓ \downarrow FLOPs ↓ \downarrow MS ↓ \downarrow MNIST C3LT 99.0 187.89 4310.01 223.86M 480.81M LeapFactual 98.6 217.08 2625.52 0.86M 56.95K CounterFlow 99.6 105.70 3224.54 174.66M 4.64M CIFAR10 C3LT 99.0 115.04 153.41 3.9